Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex pattern(R"((\*.*?)\*)");
    std::smatch matches;
    while (std::regex_replace(html, matches, pattern)) {
        html = matches[1];
    }
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to replace markdown tags with HTML tags. The regular expression pattern `(\*.*?)\*` matches any text enclosed in asterisks, which represents the markdown tags. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace the markdown tags with their corresponding HTML tags.

Here's an example usage of the function:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a *bold* text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This will output:

```
This is a <strong>bold</strong> text
```

Note that this function only handles basic markdown tags.

